  "analysis": "Current state shows a Linux shell prompt with a blank workspace. The task requires implementing a solution to compute the minimal width to fit an advertisement within k lines, given wrap rules with spaces and hyphens as wrap points. The plan is to implement a C++ program that binary searches the answer and uses a linear-time feasibility check for a given width. The check treats spaces and hyphens as valid wrap points and uses a greedy wrap approach: scan through the text, track the last wrap point, and when the current line would exceed the width, wrap at the last wrap point if possible. This handles both wrapping between words (space) and inside hyphenated words. After implementing, compile and run tests for the provided samples to verify correctness.",
  "plan": "1) Write a C++17 program that: \n   - Reads k (int) and the ad string (second line, may contain spaces and hyphens).\n   - Implements a function ok(W) that checks if the text can be wrapped within W width using at most k lines via a greedy wrap with break opportunities after spaces or hyphens.\n   - Performs binary search on width W in [1, n] where n is the total length of the ad, using ok(W) to guide the search.\n   - Outputs the minimal width.\n2) Compile the program with g++.\n3) Run the program against the sample tests to ensure outputs 7 and 10 respectively.\n4) Provide the commands batch to reproduce the solution in the terminal.",
